# ============================================================================
# LazyDev — Lazy Issue Resolver  ·  Environment configuration
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This file is the source of truth for every variable LazyDev reads.
# Copy it to `.env` and fill in the values for your deployment.
#
# Variables are grouped by purpose and tagged with one of two deployment modes:
#
#   [DOCKER]    — only relevant when running the full stack via
#                 `docker compose up`. The docker-compose.yml already sets
#                 sensible defaults for these; you usually do NOT need to
#                 touch them. They are listed here for completeness / override.
#   [DEV]       — only relevant when running the NestJS app locally
#                 (`npm run start:dev`) against containerised infra.
#
# Anything tagged BOTH is required in either mode.
# ============================================================================


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 1. GitHub App
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Create your app at https://github.com/apps/lazydev-issue-resolver (or your
# own fork). See README.md → "Installing the GitHub App" for the full guide.
# Required in BOTH Docker and Dev.

GITHUB_APP_ID=
GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# Path (relative to the project root) to the app's *.pem private key.
# Alternatively, inline the key into GITHUB_PRIVATE_KEY (newline-escaped).
GITHUB_PRIVATE_KEY_PATH=github-private-key.pem
# GITHUB_PRIVATE_KEY=-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\n...\n-----END RSA PRIVATE KEY-----


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 2. LLM provider  (agent reasoning)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Pick ONE provider below and set the variables. Direct Gemini, DeepSeek, and
# Anthropic base URLs are auto-detected and routed to the provider-NATIVE
# LangChain client (ChatGoogleGenerativeAI / ChatDeepSeek / ChatAnthropic),
# which handles their provider-specific payload requirements (Gemini
# thought_signature, DeepSeek reasoning_content) natively. Everything else
# (OpenAI, OpenRouter, NVIDIA, Z.AI, MiniMax, Xiaomi, Kimi, Grok, Ollama,
# LiteLLM/vLLM gateways) goes through ChatOpenAI's OpenAI-compatible client —
# for those, ONLY the base URL + key + model name differ.
# Required in BOTH Docker and Dev.

# ── Option A — OpenAI (native) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
OPENAI_API_KEY=
OPENAI_BASE_URL=                                   # leave blank for api.openai.com
LLM_MODEL=gpt-4o-mini

# ── Option B — Google Gemini (direct, native client) ─────────────────────────
# The old OpenAI-compat URL (…/v1beta/openai) still works — the /v1beta/openai
# suffix is simply ignored and the native Gemini endpoint is used.
# OPENAI_API_KEY=your_gemini_api_key
# OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com
# LLM_MODEL=gemini-2.5-flash

# ── Option C — OpenRouter (one key, many models) ────────────────────────────
# Routed through the OpenAI-compatible client; DeepSeek/Gemini models hosted
# on OpenRouter keep working (their round-trip fields are preserved by the
# converter patch in llm.service.ts).
# OPENAI_API_KEY=your_openrouter_key
# OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://openrouter.ai/api/v1
# LLM_MODEL=anthropic/claude-3.5-sonnet

# ── Option D — DeepSeek (direct, native client; thinking-mode round-trips
#    reasoning_content via the converter patch) ──────────────────────────────
# OPENAI_API_KEY=your_deepseek_key
# OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://api.deepseek.com
# LLM_MODEL=deepseek-chat

# ── Option E — Anthropic (direct, native Messages API client) ───────────────
# Previously only reachable via OpenRouter — now supported directly.
# OPENAI_API_KEY=your_anthropic_key
# OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://api.anthropic.com
# LLM_MODEL=claude-sonnet-4-5

# ── Option F — Ollama (fully local, free, no API key) ───────────────────────
# Leave OPENAI_API_KEY blank and set OLLAMA_* below. The app auto-falls back.
# OPENAI_API_KEY=
# OLLAMA_HOST=http://localhost:11434
# OLLAMA_LLM_MODEL=llama3
# LLM_MODEL=llama3                                   # must match OLLAMA_LLM_MODEL

# ── Options G–L — other OpenAI-compatible providers (direct, no proxy) ──────
# All of these route through the OpenAI-compatible client exactly like
# Option C — set the provider's base URL, your key, and a model name.
# NOTE: if you switch the chat LLM away from Gemini, decouple embeddings with
# EMBEDDING_API_KEY / EMBEDDING_BASE_URL (section 3) so RAG keeps working.
#
# ── Option G — NVIDIA NIM (build.nvidia.com, free trial credits) ────────────
# Models: qwen/qwen2.5-coder-32b-instruct · deepseek-ai/deepseek-r1 ·
#         meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct · nvidia/llama-3.3-nemotron-super-49b-v1
# OPENAI_API_KEY=your_nvidia_key
# OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://integrate.api.nvidia.com/v1
# LLM_MODEL=qwen/qwen2.5-coder-32b-instruct
#
# ── Option H — Z.AI (GLM; use the coding endpoint with a GLM Coding Plan) ───
# Models: glm-5.3 · glm-5.3-flash · glm-4.5-air (CN platform: bigmodel.cn)
# OPENAI_API_KEY=your_zai_key
# OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://api.z.ai/api/paas/v4        # coding plan: /api/coding/paas/v4
# LLM_MODEL=glm-5.3
#
# ── Option I — MiniMax (intl: minimax.io · CN: minimaxi.com) ────────────────
# Models: MiniMax-M3 · MiniMax-M2.7 · MiniMax-M2.7-highspeed
# OPENAI_API_KEY=your_minimax_key
# OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://api.minimax.io/v1
# LLM_MODEL=MiniMax-M3
#
# ── Option J — Xiaomi MiMo ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Models: mimo-v2.5-pro · mimo-v2.5 (reasoning returns as reasoning_content,
# preserved by the converter patch like DeepSeek's)
# OPENAI_API_KEY=your_mimo_key
# OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://api.xiaomimimo.com/v1
# LLM_MODEL=mimo-v2.5-pro
#
# ── Option K — Kimi / Moonshot (intl: moonshot.ai · CN: moonshot.cn) ────────
# Models: kimi-k3 · kimi-k2.6
# OPENAI_API_KEY=your_moonshot_key
# OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://api.moonshot.ai/v1
# LLM_MODEL=kimi-k3
#
# ── Option L — Grok / xAI ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Models: grok-4.6 · grok-4.6-fast (reasoning models benefit from long
# timeouts; the OpenAI client default of 10 min is usually enough)
# OPENAI_API_KEY=your_xai_key
# OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://api.x.ai/v1
# LLM_MODEL=grok-4.6


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 2a. BYOK — bring-your-own-key via the dashboard settings UI  (optional)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Users can submit their own provider config (apiKey + baseUrl + model) through
# PUT /api/dashboard/settings/llm instead of editing .env. Rows are stored in
# the llm_configs table, scoped by GitHub App installation:
#   - { installationId: 123, ... } → that installation's own provider
#   - installationId omitted/null → global default row for ALL installations
#     without their own row (how a self-hosted operator sets "the" provider)
# Resolution order per pipeline run: installation row → global row → env vars
# above. An effective BYOK row FULLY replaces env (including per-agent env
# overrides). API keys are encrypted at rest (AES-256-GCM) and only ever
# returned masked (last 4 chars).
#
# Master key for that encryption — REQUIRED before the BYOK API accepts keys.
# Generate:  openssl rand -base64 32
# Rotating it invalidates existing stored keys (users must re-enter them).
#LLM_CONFIG_ENCRYPTION_KEY=


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 2b. Per-agent provider + model overrides  (optional)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every agent shares OPENAI_API_KEY / OPENAI_BASE_URL / LLM_MODEL by default.
# Override any agent to use a DIFFERENT PROVIDER (its own API key + base URL)
# and/or a different model. Each override is independent — any field you leave
# unset inherits the shared default above. Provider dispatch is per-agent too:
# a role whose base URL points at Gemini/DeepSeek/Anthropic gets that
# provider's native client, while the other roles stay wherever they point.
#
# Example — onboarding on cheap DeepSeek, patcher on strong OpenAI:
#   ONBOARDING_MODEL=deepseek-chat
#   ONBOARDING_API_KEY=sk-deepseek-...
#   ONBOARDING_BASE_URL=https://api.deepseek.com
#   PATCH_GENERATOR_MODEL=gpt-4o
#   # (no PATCH_GENERATOR_API_KEY/BASE_URL → inherits shared OpenAI)
#
# Leave everything commented to use the shared default for every agent.
#
# ── PlannerAgent (research + planning) ──────────────────────────────────────
# PLANNER_MODEL=
# PLANNER_API_KEY=
# PLANNER_BASE_URL=
#
# ── PatchGeneratorAgent (the heavy lifter) ──────────────────────────────────
# PATCH_GENERATOR_MODEL=
# PATCH_GENERATOR_API_KEY=
# PATCH_GENERATOR_BASE_URL=
#
# ── ValidationAgent ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# VALIDATION_MODEL=
# VALIDATION_API_KEY=
# VALIDATION_BASE_URL=
#
# ── GitAgent (no LLM — leave blank) ─────────────────────────────────────────
# GIT_MODEL=
# GIT_API_KEY=
# GIT_BASE_URL=
#
# ── OnboardingAgent ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ONBOARDING_MODEL=
# ONBOARDING_API_KEY=
# ONBOARDING_BASE_URL=


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 3. Embedding provider  (RAG vector search)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# EMBEDDING_PROVIDER: 'google' | 'openai' | 'ollama'
# If unset, auto-detected from the effective key + base URL (see below).
EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=google

# EMBEDDING_MODEL — model name for the selected provider:
#   Google : text-embedding-004 (768-dim) · text-multilingual-embedding-002
#            gemini-embedding-2 (default)
#   OpenAI : text-embedding-3-small (1536-dim) · text-embedding-3-large (3072-dim)
#   Ollama : nomic-embed-text (768-dim) · mxbai-embed-large (1024-dim)
EMBEDDING_MODEL=gemini-embedding-2

# EMBEDDING_API_KEY / EMBEDDING_BASE_URL — decouple embeddings from the chat
# LLM. They take precedence over OPENAI_API_KEY / OPENAI_BASE_URL, so you can
# e.g. run the agents on OpenRouter (section 2, Option C) while embeddings
# stay on your Gemini key. Leave both unset to inherit the chat provider's
# credentials (previous behaviour).
# Example — agents on OpenRouter, embeddings on Gemini:
#   EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=google
#   EMBEDDING_API_KEY=your_gemini_api_key
#EMBEDDING_API_KEY=
#EMBEDDING_BASE_URL=

# Delay (ms) between embedding calls — free tier: 700ms, paid tier: 50-100ms.
EMBEDDING_RATE_LIMIT_MS=700

# Used when EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=ollama OR as a free local fallback.
OLLAMA_HOST=http://localhost:11434
OLLAMA_EMBEDDING_MODEL=nomic-embed-text


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 4. Serena MCP  (project memory & semantic code search)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Serena runs as its own container (docker-compose.yml → `serena` service).
# These flags control whether the app talks to it for durable memories.

# Set to 'true' to enable the OnboardingAgent and store project context in
# Serena MCP. Defaults to false to save tokens on ephemeral worktrees.
ENABLE_SERENA_MEMORIES=false

# Set to 'true' to track resolved issues and log them to Serena MCP.
ENABLE_SERENA_ISSUE_HISTORY=false


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 5. Notifications & Git identity
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL=                               # optional

# Git author stamped on every automated commit / PR.
GIT_USER_NAME="LazyDev Bot"
GIT_USER_EMAIL="lazydev@futureminds.dev"

# Git push strategy for the agent's fix/feat branch:
#   'force' (DEFAULT)        — overwrite remote branch if it exists (destructive)
#   'unique_branch'          — append timestamp to branch name (never conflicts)
#   'standard'               — plain push (fails if branch diverged)
GIT_PUSH_STRATEGY=force


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 6. LazyDev MCP Server  (for Hermes / OpenClaw / Claude Desktop / …)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Exposes trigger_issue_fix, implement_new_feature, get_pipeline_status and
# provide_human_feedback to any MCP client. Enabled by default.
# Set to 'false' to switch the MCP surface off entirely.
# MCP_SERVER_ENABLED=true

# MCP is served on the main API port at POST /mcp (shared with the public API).
# This is the only mode supported by the self-hosted build.

# Optional bearer token. When set, MCP clients must send
# `Authorization: Bearer <token>`. Strongly recommended — without it, anyone
# who can reach port 3200 can spend your AI compute.
# MCP_AUTH_TOKEN=change-me


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 7. Sandbox & worktree cleanup
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Network mode for the Docker validation sandbox sibling container:
#   'bridge' (DEFAULT) — sandbox has internet (needed for npm install)
#   'none'              — fully isolated (high security; may break installs)
SANDBOX_NETWORK_MODE=bridge

# On startup, LazyDev removes worktree directories under WORKTREE_BASE_PATH
# that are older than this many hours and have no in-memory record (left
# behind by a process restart mid-job). Default: 6.
# WORKTREE_ORPHAN_MAX_AGE_HOURS=6


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 8. Infrastructure  (Docker-internal — usually leave defaults)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# These are [DOCKER]-internal: docker-compose.yml already sets DB_HOST=postgres,
# REDIS_HOST=redis, QDRANT_URL=http://qdrant:6333, etc. Only touch them if you
# are running the app locally ([DEV]) or pointing at external infra.

# [DEV]  Database (PostgreSQL)
DB_HOST=localhost
DB_PORT=5432
DB_USER=postgres
DB_PASSWORD=postgres
DB_NAME=lazy_issue_resolver

# [DEV]  Redis (BullMQ queue)
REDIS_HOST=localhost
REDIS_PORT=6379

# [BOTH]  HTTP server port. Avoid 3000 — commonly taken by other local agent
#         tooling (e.g. the Hermes WhatsApp bridge).
PORT=3200
NODE_ENV=development

# [DEV]  Repository cache directory (clones live here). In Docker this is the
#        `repo-cache` named volume mounted at /app/repo-cache — do not set.
REPO_CACHE_DIR=./repo-cache
LAZYDEV_DEFAULT_BRANCH=main


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 9. Dashboard control-plane API  (consumed by the lazydev-frontend UI)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The dashboard UI (separate repo, lazydev-frontend) calls this backend's
# /api/dashboard/* and /api/auth/* endpoints. These vars drive the meta,
# settings, grafana, and auth endpoints. Required in BOTH Docker and Dev
# whenever the UI is in use.

# Comma-separated list of allowed CORS origins for the dashboard UI.
# Default covers local Next.js dev (3000). In Docker the frontend is usually
# served by the `frontend` service on its own port — add that origin too.
# Include 127.0.0.1 variants if you access the frontend via 127.0.0.1
# (browsers treat localhost and 127.0.0.1 as DIFFERENT origins for CORS).
# Example: http://localhost:3000,http://127.0.0.1:3000,http://localhost:3001
CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000,http://127.0.0.1:3000

# Deployment mode drives GET /api/dashboard/meta:
#   selfhosted (DEFAULT) — single-tenant, no auth, all nav items visible
DEPLOYMENT_MODE=selfhosted

# Auth mode shown to the frontend (returned by /meta and /api/auth/session):
#   none         (DEFAULT) — no login, sidebar hides user UI
#   token        — static bearer token (set DASHBOARD_AUTH_TOKEN)
DASHBOARD_AUTH=none
# DASHBOARD_AUTH_TOKEN=change-me          # required when DASHBOARD_AUTH=token

# Public URL of the frontend dashboard (used for redirects). In dev this is the
# Next.js dev server; in Docker it is the frontend service URL.
FRONTEND_URL=http://localhost:3000

# ── Grafana embed config (GET /api/dashboard/grafana) ───────────────────────
# Leave GRAFANA_BASE_URL blank to disable embeds (frontend handles gracefully).
# In Docker, these are set in docker-compose.yml — only set them here if you
# run the backend outside Docker or want to override the Docker defaults.
# GRAFANA_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3100
# JSON array of {uid,title,embedUrl,description?} dashboards. The embedUrl
# must be reachable from the browser (host port, not Docker network).
# GRAFANA_DASHBOARDS=[{"uid":"lazydev-overview","title":"Backend Overview","embedUrl":"http://localhost:3100/d-solo/lazydev-overview/backend-overview?panelId=1&from=now-1h&to=now&theme=dark&kiosk=tv","description":"Memory, event loop, and GC metrics"}]


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 10. Webhook tunnel  (ngrok — for GitHub App webhooks without a public IP)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# GitHub needs a PUBLIC URL to deliver webhook events. The docker-compose.yml
# runs an ngrok container that tunnels a public URL to the app on port 3200,
# so you don't need ngrok installed on your host.
#
# 1. Sign up at https://ngrok.com and get your authtoken
#    (https://dashboard.ngrok.com/get-started/your-authtoken).
# 2. Set NGROK_AUTHTOKEN below.
# 3. Start the stack WITH the tunnel profile:
#      docker compose --profile tunnel up
#    (plain `docker compose up` skips ngrok — useful for local-only testing.)
#    NOTE: --profile is a top-level flag — it goes BEFORE `up`, not after.
# 4. The public URL is printed in `docker compose logs ngrok`
#    (look for "Forwarding https://<random>.ngrok-free.app").
# 5. Paste that URL + /webhooks/github into your GitHub App's Webhook URL
#    (https://github.com/apps/lazydev-issue-resolver → General settings).
#
# Free tier gives a random URL each restart. For a stable URL, reserve a
# domain (paid plan) and set NGROK_DOMAIN.
NGROK_AUTHTOKEN=
# NGROK_DOMAIN=your-reserved-domain.ngrok-free.app
